# Password-gating the whole Harness, which the plugin cannot do for you.
#
# The plugin's own lock (Settings -> Privacy) covers the Local Agents panel and is
# enforced on the Host, so it cannot be walked around. It does not cover the rest
# of the Harness: a plugin has no seat in front of DSH's HTTP routes — the
# `/api` transport belongs to another plugin, registering a duplicate path throws,
# the fallback seat is already taken by the SPA, and there is no middleware hook.
# Gating the Harness therefore has to happen in front of it, which is this file.
#
# What this gives you:
#   - One password for everything served at this address, checked before any
#     request reaches DSH.
#   - TLS, so the password and everything after it is not sent in the clear. This
#     is the part the plugin's lock cannot provide and the reason it tells you so.
#   - WebSocket forwarding, which the Harness needs: its browser transport uses
#     /api/events.mux and /api/events.host. Caddy proxies upgrades by default, so
#     there is nothing extra to write here — but if you adapt this to nginx, that
#     is the line people forget.
#
# Usage:
#   1. Hash your password:  caddy hash-password
#      It prints a bcrypt hash. Paste it below. Do not put the password itself
#      here; this file is meant to be readable.
#   2. Replace harness.example.internal with a name that resolves to this machine.
#      For a Tailscale or WireGuard address, use that name — the point is that the
#      address is reachable only from your own network.
#   3. caddy run --config Caddyfile
#   4. Leave DSH bound to 127.0.0.1. Caddy reaches it over loopback; nothing else
#      can. If you change the bind address, this whole file stops being a fence.
#
# What this is not: a multi-user system. One shared password identifies nobody. If
# you need to know who did what, put an OIDC or mTLS proxy here instead — the shape
# of the file is the same, the auth directive is what changes.

harness.example.internal {
	# A private CA is fine here and avoids a public DNS challenge. Swap for a real
	# certificate if this name is publicly resolvable.
	tls internal

	basic_auth {
		# operator <bcrypt hash from `caddy hash-password`>
		operator $2a$14$REPLACE_THIS_WITH_YOUR_OWN_HASH_OR_NOBODY_IS_KEPT_OUT
	}

	# Loopback only. DSH must not be listening anywhere else.
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3080 {
		# The Harness reads the Host header to decide whether a request looks like a
		# rebinding attack, so it has to see the name the browser used. Caddy passes
		# it through by default; this is here so that stays true if someone adds
		# header_up rules below it.
		header_up Host {host}
	}
}
